.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt PIVOT_ROOT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pivot_root
.Nd change the root mount
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_pivot_root "const char *new_root" "const char *put_old"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn pivot_root
makes the root of the mount
.Fa new_root
names the root directory of the calling process, and shows the old root
at
.Fa put_old ,
a directory at or below
.Fa new_root .
Both paths are resolved from the caller's root and working directory
before the call changes anything.
.Pp
The old root is shown at
.Fa put_old
by a bind of it with the mounts below it, all but
.Fa new_root
and those below that, and the original mounts are taken from the
caller's mount namespace, as Linux moves them; the new root and the
mounts it is below stay, so that a descriptor opened on one of them before
the call still reaches it.
.Xr umount2 2
of
.Fa put_old
with
.Dv MNT_DETACH
then lets go of the old root; see
.Xr mount 2 .
Other mount namespaces see no change.
.Pp
When
.Fa put_old
names
.Fa new_root
itself, as bubblewrap, runc and LXC call it with
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql \&. ,
the old root is shown nowhere and no mount is taken.
On Linux it is stacked over the new root; the caller lets go of it by
.Xr fchdir 2
to a descriptor of the old root opened before, and
.Xr umount2 2
of
.Ql \&.
with
.Dv MNT_DETACH ,
which here takes the mounts below the old root but the caller's new root
and those below it.
.Pp
Every process of the caller's mount namespace whose root was the old root
has the new root after the call, the caller among them.
A working directory keeps its place: its path is then given from the new
root, or, for one outside the new root, it is outside the root, as after
.Xr fchdir 2
to such a directory, and
.Xr getcwd 2
fails with
.Er ENOENT .
.Pp
The i386 system call number is 217, with
.Fa new_root
in EBX and
.Fa put_old
in ECX.
On x86-64 the number is 155, with the arguments in RDI and RSI.
Musl has no wrapper, and a program calls
.Xr syscall 2 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn pivot_root
returns 0 on success, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/musl/sandboxmusl.c
builds a root as bubblewrap does, in a mount namespace of its own: a
tmpfs, a bind of its
.Pa newroot
over itself,
.Fn pivot_root
to the tmpfs with the old root at
.Pa oldroot ,
a lazy unmount of
.Pa oldroot ,
and
.Fn pivot_root
of
.Ql \&.
and
.Ql \&.
in
.Pa newroot .
It checks that the probe's own directory cannot be written from the new
root, that a file made there is not seen outside, and that a program
without
.Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN
is refused the call.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EBUSY
.Fa new_root
or
.Fa put_old
is on the mount of the caller's root.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A path is outside the caller's memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa new_root
is not the root of a mount, is a permanent mount or one of the synthetic
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc
or
.Pa /sys ;
the caller's root is not the root of a mount, as after
.Xr chroot 2
to a directory; or
.Fa put_old
is not at or below
.Fa new_root .
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A path is too long, or the path of
.Fa put_old
exceeds the 255 bytes of a mount's target.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A directory of a path does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel had no memory for the paths or the bind.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
.Fa put_old ,
or a component of a path, is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller is not privileged over its mount namespace: it lacks
.Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN
in the user namespace that owns it; see
.Xr unshare 2
and
.Xr capget 2 .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr capget 2 ,
.Xr chroot 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr prctl 2 ,
.Xr unshare 2
